Replace the oracle's evaluation with the same material sum MCFISH_EVAL_MATERIAL uses.

This is the OTHER half of the spine-isolation workload. docs/09-tooling-ci.md has
said "patch the oracle with the same formula" since the knob landed, but shipped no
patch, so every spine measurement re-derived it by hand -- and the whole point of
writing the weights out in both engines is that the two cannot drift. A recipe in
prose can drift; a patch cannot.

The formula is src/engine/search/search_common.c's material_only_eval: piece values
{P 100, N 320, B 330, R 500, Q 900}, from the side to move's point of view, with NO
optimism blend, NO rule50 damping and NO tablebase clamp. Every one of those would
be a divergence, and the node counts would stop matching -- which is exactly how you
find out you got it wrong. ASSERT THE NODE COUNTS MATCH BEFORE BELIEVING ANYTHING
the resulting pair measures.

DO NOT APPLY THIS BY HAND. `./build.sh material-eval [arch]` does the whole
sequence, and it exists for the RESTORE rather than the apply.

Reverting the source is NOT enough. The last thing built is the STUBBED oracle and
it stays on disk; every in-repo consumer routes through upstream_oracle.sh, which
would rebuild -- except the `.built-sha` stamp still matches the pin, so it sees a
current build and does nothing, and anyone running the oracle binary directly then
measures a stub. That happened here during the c5aef2bf1 spine run: the restore ran
from the wrong directory, exited 127, and the oracle benched 4186134 instead of the
anchor until it was caught by hand. The script's EXIT trap deletes the binary AND
the stamp as well as reverting the source, so a missing oracle fails loudly on the
next use where a stubbed one would not.

It also asserts the two trees match at two depths before handing the pair over,
which is the only thing that can catch the two formulas drifting apart.

--- a/src/evaluate.cpp
+++ b/src/evaluate.cpp
@@ -46,6 +46,21 @@ Value Eval::evaluate(const Eval::NNUE::Network&     network,

     assert(!pos.checkers());

+    // SPINE ISOLATION (mcfish tools/material_eval.patch): material sum only.
+    // Mirrors mcfish's material_only_eval exactly -- same weights, same point of
+    // view, and none of the blending below it. Returning early leaves the rest of
+    // this function dead on purpose, so the diff stays one hunk and reverts clean.
+    {
+        static const int matWeight[QUEEN + 1] = {0, 100, 320, 330, 500, 900};
+        const Color      matUs                = pos.side_to_move();
+        const Color      matThem              = ~matUs;
+        int              matV                 = 0;
+        for (PieceType pt = PAWN; pt <= QUEEN; ++pt)
+            matV += matWeight[pt]
+                  * (popcount(pos.pieces(matUs, pt)) - popcount(pos.pieces(matThem, pt)));
+        return Value(matV);
+    }
+
     auto [psqt, positional] = network.evaluate(pos, accumulators, caches);

     Value nnue = psqt + positional;
